What upstream lacks (hermes-agent, apps/desktop transcript): a reader who
scrolls up while the agent works is not left alone.

The transcript's follow state belongs to use-stick-to-bottom. Only an upward
WHEEL escapes it synchronously; every other scroll (scrollbar drag, PageUp,
keyboard) goes through the library's scroll handler, which ignores the event
while a content resize is in flight, which during a stream is almost every
frame. The reader stays locked and the next streamed token pins them back to
the bottom (measured: 1.8-2.4k px yanks within a second of a scrollbar
scroll-up). The library also re-locks on any downward scroll or shrink that
lands within 70 px of the bottom, and native overflow-anchor sometimes
anchored a node inside a re-rendering block of the live turn, moving the text
being read by ~118 px back and forth.

Even with the wheel, every turn end moved what was being read: the finished
turn was REMOUNTED. Its row key changes twice when it ends (the stream
message gets a stored rowId, then the history re-read replaces it with a
stored message with another id and often another rowId), and inside the turn
the prompt/response sections are keyed by message id, which every history
re-read renames. A remount replaces every node under the reader and lays the
turn out afresh (measured 83-166 px at each turn end; 916 px when the
auto-continued turn after a background-process notice started).

PageUp with the composer focused (where it is after sending) did nothing:
the composer swallows the key and the global keybind skips bare navigation
keys in an editable target.

This patch (Nix) adds thread/reading-anchor.ts and thread/stable-row-keys.ts,
used by thread/list.tsx and thread/response-group.tsx:
  - a follow state machine changed only by user input: an upward wheel or a
    scroll made while the user is touching an input (pointer held on the
    viewport/scrollbar, touch, wheel, PageUp/PageDown, other nav keys only
    with the viewport focused, not typed in the composer) stops following;
    the scrollTop clamp of content shrinking at the bottom (a row collapsed
    by a click) is not a scroll up. A user scroll down to the bottom, "Jump
    to latest" or any scrollToBottom call resumes it. Content resizes never
    change it; a library lock taken while reading is undone, a stopScroll
    elsewhere (inline edit, PageUp, restored offset) is adopted as reading;
    re-taking a lost lock skips a transcript text selection (#115464);
  - while reading, the element under the reading line (30 % from the top,
    sticky overlays skipped) and the box right after it are remembered with
    their on-screen top, and after every transcript layout change
    (ResizeObserver on the content and each row, before paint) scrollTop is
    corrected by however far the first still-visible one moved, so rows
    growing/collapsing above and output added below leave it in place; a
    scroll that is neither the user's nor the anchor's and comes with a
    height change (old rows dropping off the front of the render window
    while the reader scrolls up, whose scroll event can run before the
    ResizeObserver) is corrected against the saved anchor too instead of
    being adopted as the new position (measured: one-off 469-1506 px jumps);
    native overflow-anchor is off meanwhile (it moved content without a
    resize callback to correct);
  - row keys stay the ones a message was first rendered under across
    persistence and history re-reads (by renderer id, stored rowId, or the
    message it replaced in place; always unique), and the prompt/response
    sections inside a turn are keyed by position (the messages in them by
    their row key, not their index, which shifts when older rows drop off),
    so a finished turn is updated in place instead of remounted, also when
    the re-read splits its assistant into two messages;
  - a programmatic scroll is announced to the anchor (event
    hermes:programmatic-scroll: the timeline rail's jump, find-in-page's
    scrollIntoView of the active match on a find or step the user made, the
    jump button revealing an approval) and suspends its corrections until no
    announcement or scroll event for 150 ms, or until the user's own wheel or
    pointer, then the anchor re-samples there and stays reading; without it
    a streaming resize in the jump's frame (before its scroll event) read the
    jump as drift and undid it. Find-in-page re-wrapping its marks after a
    re-render (every token while the bar is open) neither reveals nor
    announces: it would pause the anchor for the whole stream;
  - "Jump to latest" resumes following also with a transcript text
    selection; requestScrollToBottom takes { explicit } so automatic callers
    (a clarify request) keep the selection pin;
  - PageUp/PageDown in the composer page the transcript (while still bound
    to conversation.scrollPageUp/Down);
  - list.tsx's own resize pin and focus re-anchor run only while following;
  - the hook's controller keeps its identity across renders: list.tsx's
    restore effect (which owns the jump button's instant path) depends on
    it, and a fresh object per render tore that path down while streaming,
    so the button fell back to the spring scroll and stopped ~30 px short.
Tested by thread/reading-anchor.test.ts, thread/stable-row-keys.test.ts and
lib/find-in-page-scope.test.ts.

